"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound** \u2013 I prove that in any admissible configuration at most one line can be sunny.  \n   * I show that if a vertical line \\(x=c\\) is missing from the family of lines, then the two points \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,2)\\) must be covered by two different sunny lines (a sunny line can meet a given vertical line in at most one point).  \n   * Repeating the argument for the smallest missing vertical line forces the existence of at least two distinct sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that there are two or more sunny lines.  \n   * Hence every admissible configuration contains **at most one** sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\)** \u2013  \n   * The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n     \\]\n\n     are all non\u2011sunny and together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   * Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=1\\)** \u2013  \n   * Keep the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) (all non\u2011sunny).  \n   * Replace the vertical line \\(x=n\\) (which contains only the single point \\((n,1)\\)) by any sunny line passing through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n     \\[\n     L_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y=\\frac12(x-n)+1 .\n     \\]\n\n   * The new line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is sunny.  \n   * All required points are still covered, because every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), and the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the sunny line.  \n   * Hence a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nCombining the upper bound with the two constructions yields the full answer.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that have to be covered.  \nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by \\(\\operatorname{slope}(L)\\) its slope; a line is **sunny** iff  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(L)\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At most one sunny line can appear\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and that at least two of its lines are sunny.  \nWe shall obtain a contradiction.\n\nFor an integer \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n)\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;c+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the vertical column of points with abscissa \\(c\\); \\(|V_c|=n+1-c\\).\n\nA sunny line can intersect a given vertical line in **at most one** point, because two distinct points with the same abscissa would force the line to be vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), which is not sunny.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nc_0=\\min\\{c\\mid V_c\\text{ is **not** a line of }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince the family contains only \\(n\\) lines but there are \\(n\\) different vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\), such a \\(c_0\\) exists.  \nBecause \\(c_0\\) is the smallest missing column, we have \\(c_0\\ge 2\\) (the column \\(V_1\\) must be present, otherwise the point \\((1,1)\\) could not be covered).\n\nThe column \\(V_{c_0}\\) contains at least the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(c_0,1),\\qquad P_2=(c_0,2).\n\\]\n\nNeither of them lies on a vertical line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (by definition of \\(c_0\\)), hence each of them must belong to a **different** sunny line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least two distinct sunny lines.\n\nNow consider the next column \\(V_{c_0+1}\\).  \nBoth sunny lines that already contain \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) intersect \\(V_{c_0+1}\\) in at most one point each; therefore at most two points of \\(V_{c_0+1}\\) are already covered.  \nSince \\(|V_{c_0+1}|=n-c_0\\ge 2\\) (because \\(c_0\\le n-1\\)), there remains at least one uncovered point of \\(V_{c_0+1}\\).  \nThat point cannot lie on a vertical line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (all such lines have abscissa \\(\\le c_0\\)), so it must be covered by a **third** sunny line, contradicting the assumption that there are only two sunny lines.\n\nHence a configuration satisfying the statement can contain **at most one** sunny line:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le 1 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach line is non\u2011sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered and the family contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction with \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\n\\]\n\nand replace the line \\(x=n\\) (which would contain only the point \\((n,1)\\)) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y=\\frac12\\,(x-n)+1 .\n\\]\n\n*The line \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is sunny.*\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThe remaining point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\).  \nHence all required points are covered, and exactly one line of the family is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFrom Section\u202f1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le 1\\).  \nSections\u202f2 and\u202f3 show that both possibilities \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\,1\\}} .\n\\]"

